1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:12,200
Getting from a C file to a running program takes four steps.

2
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:18,533
Compiler, Assembler, Linker, Loader: put their first letters together and you get CALL.

3
00:00:18,533 --> 00:00:25,300
The compiler translates C into assembly: exactly what we've been doing by hand in earlier episodes.

4
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:32,900
The compiler's output may use pseudo-instructions such as mv, li and j; the assembler expands them.

5
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:41,333
The assembler reads assembly code and produces an object file. Besides machine code, it holds information for linking and debugging.

6
00:00:41,333 --> 00:00:46,900
Job one: expand pseudo-instructions into real instructions.

7
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:52,733
Job two: turn labels into offsets. But there's a snag: forward references.

8
00:00:52,733 --> 00:00:59,533
When the assembler reaches bne, it hasn't seen Skip defined yet, so it can't tell how far to jump.

9
00:00:59,533 --> 00:01:08,033
The fix: two passes. The first pass generates no machine code; it just records each label's address in the symbol table.

10
00:01:08,033 --> 00:01:18,100
The second pass generates the machine code, now knowing where Skip is: offset = 0x08 − 0x00 = 8.

11
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:26,666
But some addresses can't be worked out in any number of passes: calling printf in another file, or taking the address of static data A.

12
00:01:26,666 --> 00:01:36,100
Their final addresses aren't known until link time, so the assembler lists these holes in the relocation table: a to-do list for the linker.

13
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:45,866
So an object file holds a header, a text segment, a data segment, a relocation table, a symbol table and debugging information.

14
00:01:45,866 --> 00:01:50,433
The linker combines several object files into one executable.

15
00:01:50,433 --> 00:01:55,500
Step 1: concatenate the text segments, then append the data segments.

16
00:01:55,500 --> 00:02:06,800
Step 2: with the segments laid out from 0x10000 up, every symbol has its final address. printf is at 0x10180.

17
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:15,966
Step 3: fill every hole in the relocation table. The jal at 0x10040 in foo must reach printf…

18
00:02:15,966 --> 00:02:27,100
…and since jal is PC-relative, the offset filled in is 0x10180 − 0x10040 = 0x140.

19
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:36,866
Branches inside a file need no changes: PC-relative offsets stay correct when the whole block moves. That's the position independence from Episode 11.

20
00:02:36,866 --> 00:02:46,800
By the way, modern systems often use dynamic linking, where libraries are linked in only when the program is loaded. What we've shown is static linking.

21
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:51,366
Finally, the operating system's loader actually gets the executable running.

22
00:02:51,366 --> 00:02:57,866
It reads the header to learn how big the text and data segments are, then creates a new address space…

23
00:02:57,866 --> 00:03:01,266
…copies the code and data into memory…

24
00:03:01,266 --> 00:03:08,100
…puts the command-line arguments on the stack, initializes the registers, and points sp at the top of the stack…

25
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:14,133
…and finally jumps to a start-up routine, which calls main. The program is running!

26
00:03:14,133 --> 00:03:21,400
We've now traveled the whole road: from C code to assembly, to machine code, to a program running in memory.

27
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:32,800
Registers, memory, branches, function calls, instruction encoding, compiling and linking: that's the big picture of RISC-V in CS61C.

